{
	title: 'Web tabs',
	spec: `The web page shows the host's tabs (task 0a) and one of them. The address is /<sessionId>, so a tab can be linked and Back/Forward move between tabs: choosing a tab pushes a history entry, and the page landing on a tab it did not ask for (the tab closed, an unknown id) replaces it instead. With no valid id in the address, the page shows the tab it showed last (localStorage), else the first tab, else asks tab-start. The host serves the page at any path shaped like a session id; GET /session goes away.
A tab strip (its layout is task 3k: one row of equal cells, paged, with a menu button opening a sheet that lists every tab with a close button) shows each tab with its marker (task fr) and has a new tab button (in the shown tab's cwd); tabs are links (<a href>), so Cmd- or middle-click opens one in a browser tab. The strip never wraps and never scrolls sideways (it would fight the back-swipe gesture). Keys where the browser lets the page have them: Alt-1..9 and Alt-0, Ctrl-N/P and Ctrl-T/W on macOS (on other systems the browser keeps Ctrl-T/W/N; the buttons do it). Each tab keeps its own composer draft (already per session) and scroll position while another is shown.
Test the router and the key mapping as plain modules without a browser; check the strip and sheet in a real browser at phone and desktop widths.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'old-router.ts (with its test) is a 92-line router with no dependency; old-web.md in task 1b explains why not Solid Router or TanStack Router. old-SessionTabs.tsx is the old strip and sheet; old-shortcuts.ts the old key mapping.',
		"Old lesson (improve-web.md): horizontal scrolling tab strips collide with the browser's back/forward swipe; wrap on desktop, a sheet on phones.",
		'Implemented: src/web/router.ts (address, history, last tab in localStorage, pick), src/web/shortcuts.ts (keys), tab switching in src/web/tabs.ts (show, onTabs, onPopState), components/Tabs.tsx, per-tab scroll places in scroll.ts. The session-id shape lives in common/session.ts (session.isId), shared by host/web.ts and the router.',
		'tab-start gained an optional cwd: the browser knows no cwd, so it sends tab-start { last? } and any open tab counts (a new one goes in host.cwd()). The page then picks the tab itself from the tabs event (address, else localStorage, else first; a closed shown tab gives way to its neighbour), since tab-start only knows one `last`. It re-sends tab-start on every connect, because the host sends tabs only to a client that starts.',
		'The strip reuses the theme class `tab` (the terminal tab colours: --active-fg, --inactive-fg, --warning-fg) on its root, so no colours are written in src/web. Marker classes avoid theme names (working, asking, attention), since e.g. `.warning` carries a background.',
		'Tab switching lives in src/web/tabs.ts (state in app.state: tabs, shown, asked), not app.ts, which would pass the 400-line limit; app.ts hands it tab events, connects, keys and popstate. The import cycle app.ts <-> tabs.ts is safe: neither uses the other at import time.',
		"Switching tabs must not carry card state across: the transcript's rows are keyed by position (task 4s), so the new tab's items bind to the cards of the tab before, and a card open there shows the other tab's item at the same place open. Open state belongs to (session, item); switching away and back leaves each card as it was, and the 4s render test covers a switch.",
		'Old Hal bug (commit f359155): the browser applied events only to the shown session, so a tab shown mid-stream rendered just the suffix received after the switch. hal2 re-opens a tab on show and folds its fresh snapshot; whatever caches another tab must apply every event to it, or drop it and re-open.',
		'Chrome repro (603 cards, an image 60% up): rendering every row took ~860 ms for a preloaded tab, and an unprepared tab showed blank then a 260 ms fade of every card. Rendering the newest 40 rows takes ~125 ms. The image loading late moved the view for one frame (scrollTop short of the new max) before reflow restored it; with fewer rows above this is bounded, and not otherwise fixed.'
